[method]
The paper used an in vivo metformin treatment model in genetically engineered mice to assess metformin's effects on chronic pancreatitis and pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma progression. KC and KPC mice were used, chronic pancreatitis was induced, and metformin-treated and control animals were compared. Pancreatic tissues were processed for histology, immunohistochemistry, and Masson's trichrome staining to evaluate precursor lesions, fibrosis, proliferation, and STAT3 signaling; survival and tumor burden were also assessed. This method is related to the research plan's focus on metformin's antiproliferative and metabolic effects in pancreatic cancer, but it is not the proposed BFB biotinylated biguanide probe approach and does not involve KP-4 cells, AMPK phosphorylation, streptavidin-based mitochondrial localization, or ATP5I targeting.
[results]
The available section headings indicate that metformin suppressed precursor lesion formation in KC mice, suppressed chronic pancreatitis-induced pancreatic tumorigenesis, reduced fibrosis in mice with chronic pancreatitis, impaired STAT3 signaling and inhibited proliferation, and reduced tumor burden while prolonging survival in KPC mice. The supplied text does not provide quantitative values, effect sizes, sample sizes, or statistical significance for these outcomes.
[context]
