- venue: Genome Biology
- citation count: 2463
[method]
The paper describes MAGeCK, a statistical analysis method for CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens. It takes sgRNA read counts from control and treated samples, normalizes read counts, models the mean-variance relationship, tests individual sgRNAs for selection, and aggregates sgRNA-level evidence to gene-level scores using modified robust rank aggregation (α-RRA). This is directly relevant to the proposed genome-wide CRISPR screens in NALM-6 cells treated with metformin, rotenone, or oligomycin A, because the proposed plan will also rely on sgRNA frequency changes to identify genes whose knockout enhances or suppresses drug effects and to compare genetic interaction profiles.
[results]
The paper reports that MAGeCK outperformed the comparison methods RIGER, RSA, edgeR, and DESeq in detecting significantly selected sgRNAs and genes in CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screen datasets. It also reports robust performance when sequencing depth was reduced and when the number of sgRNAs per gene was varied, and it identified known and novel biologically interesting genes and pathways in a leukemia dataset. The supplied excerpt does not provide specific quantitative values such as AUC, precision, recall, p-values, or fold changes.
[context]
The method was evaluated computationally on CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screen datasets, including a leukemia dataset, and was compared with RIGER, RSA, edgeR, and DESeq. Robustness was assessed by sgRNA down-sampling and sequencing read down-sampling, and the method was also run on RNAi screening data. The excerpt does not specify the exact cell line, treatment conditions, sequencing depth, library size, number of sgRNAs per gene, or software parameters, so those details cannot be confirmed from the provided text.
[end related work 1]

