[method]
The paper used a CRISPR/Cas9 metabolic library screen in non-small cell lung cancer cells under AURKA inhibition to identify genes whose loss altered drug response. sgRNA abundance was tracked by sequencing to detect genes that were selected against or favored during AURKA inhibitor treatment. Candidate hits were then examined through functional validation, including KEAP1 deficiency, assessment of NRF2 activation, eIF2α-ATF4-ASNS pathway regulation, apoptosis, AURKA interaction with GCN2 and eIF2α, and ASNS dependence. This approach is methodologically related to the proposed research method because both use CRISPR-based genetic perturbation under drug treatment and sgRNA frequency changes to identify genes that enhance or suppress drug effects and to infer mechanistic relationships. However, the paper’s screen was performed in NSCLC cells with AURKA inhibition, not in NALM-6 cells with metformin, rotenone, or oligomycin A, and it does not directly address ATP5I or biguanide mechanisms.
[results]
The CRISPR/Cas9 metabolic screen identified KEAP1 as a significantly negatively selected gene during AURKA inhibition, indicating that KEAP1 loss increased sensitivity to AURKA inhibition. KEAP1 deficiency activated NRF2 and intensified AURKA-inhibition-mediated downregulation of the eIF2α-ATF4-ASNS pathway and apoptosis. AURKA was reported to interact with GCN2 and eIF2α and to regulate their phosphorylation. KEAP1-deficient NSCLC cells showed dependence on ASNS, which was linked to their increased sensitivity to AURKA inhibition. The provided excerpt does not include numerical fold changes, p-values, IC50 values, screen duration, or statistical thresholds.
[context]
The experiments were conducted in NSCLC cell models using a CRISPR/Cas9 metabolic library and AURKA inhibition as the selective pressure. Supporting methods included plasmid construction, RNA extraction and qRT-PCR, RNA sequencing, colony formation assays, cell viability assays, apoptosis analysis, western blot, co-immunoprecipitation, in vitro kinase assay, mouse experiments, bioinformatics analysis, and statistical analysis. The excerpt does not specify the AURKA inhibitor identity, concentration, sgRNA library coverage, sequencing depth, screen duration, or statistical criteria, so it cannot be used to infer biguanide-, ATP5I-, or NALM-6-specific experimental conditions.
[end related work 3]

