  "support": "The strongest support is from [(Li et al., 2014-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3fcd6a0ae6a73c4e196dc13a994d7f12514fc53d), which provides MAGeCK, a statistical framework specifically for pooled CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens that takes sgRNA read counts from control and treated samples, normalizes counts, models the mean-variance relationship, tests sgRNA-level selection, and aggregates sgRNA evidence to gene-level scores using modified robust rank aggregation, α-RRA. This directly supports the proposed NALM-6 metformin, rotenone, and oligomycin A screens because the plan depends on sgRNA frequency changes and gene-level comparison of genetic interaction profiles. Li et al. also report that MAGeCK outperformed RIGER, RSA, edgeR, and DESeq in detecting selected sgRNAs and genes, and remained robust when sequencing depth or sgRNAs per gene were reduced, which supports using a rank-based, depth-robust pipeline for the proposed screens. The leukemia dataset context is especially relevant to NALM-6, although the excerpt does not give AUC, precision, recall, p-values, fold changes, exact sequencing depth, library size, or parameters. The next strongest support is [(Haldrup et al., 2023-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3e2f525ff4f082d565654d86a8e72306d2e1fa1a), which used pooled genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens in C4 mCRPC cells under enzalutamide, docetaxel, or cabazitaxel, selected top hits by sgRNA representation or gene rank, and validated hits with single-gene knockout lines, dose-response and viability or proliferation assays, RT-qPCR, QuantSeq transcriptomics, and Reactome pathway analysis. This supports the proposed workflow of drug-conditioned CRISPR screens followed by functional and transcriptomic validation, and its PRKAB1 or AMPK alpha 1 context is directly relevant to metformin-induced AMPK activation, although the system is prostate cancer rather than NALM-6 leukemia and the excerpt lacks drug concentrations, screen duration, library size, sequencing depth, and statistical thresholds. The weakest but still relevant support is [(Deng et al., 2024-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3c1094cbe0489538a05dbfae684364d473197081), which used a CRISPR/Cas9 metabolic library screen in NSCLC cells under AURKA inhibition, identified KEAP1 as negatively selected, and linked KEAP1 loss to NRF2 activation, eIF2α-ATF4-ASNS pathway downregulation, apoptosis, ASNS dependence, and AURKA-GCN2-eIF2α interactions. This supports the broader strategy of using CRISPR metabolic screens under drug pressure to infer pathway-level mechanisms, but it is less directly relevant because it does not involve NALM-6, metformin, rotenone, oligomycin A, ATP5I, or biguanide-specific targets, and it lacks numerical fold changes, p-values, IC50 values, screen duration, or statistical thresholds.",
  "contradictions": "The main contradiction is not a direct refutation but a lack of direct evidence that the proposed NALM-6, metformin, rotenone, and oligomycin A CRISPR design will identify ATP5I-related biguanide mechanisms. None of the related works tested NALM-6 cells, medicinal biguanides, complex I inhibition by rotenone, or complex V inhibition by oligomycin A in the same screen, so they cannot confirm that growth-inhibitory concentrations of these compounds will generate sufficient sgRNA selection for MAGeCK-style analysis. The strongest limitation is that [(Li et al., 2014-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3fcd6a0ae6a73c4e196dc13a994d7f12514fc53d) validates MAGeCK on CRISPR knockout screen datasets and a leukemia dataset, but the excerpt does not specify the exact cell line, treatment, sequencing depth, library size, sgRNAs per gene, or statistical parameters, and it does not address biguanide or respiratory-chain inhibitor screens. This leaves uncertainty about whether the same mean-variance and α-RRA assumptions hold for sublethal, growth-inhibitory metformin, rotenone, or oligomycin A conditions, where selection may be weaker and confounded by proliferation rate. The next limitation is that [(Haldrup et al., 2023-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3e2f525ff4f082d565654d86a8e72306d2e1fa1a) supports drug-conditioned CRISPR screens and AMPK-related PRKAB1 validation, but it was performed in C4 mCRPC cells with enzalutamide, docetaxel, or cabazitaxel, not in NALM-6 leukemia cells with metformin, rotenone, or oligomycin A, and it does not report quantitative sgRNA enrichment, p-values, IC50 values, transcriptome fold changes, or the effect of PRKAB1 loss on drug response. Therefore it supports the workflow but does not establish that cross-compound genetic interaction profiling will be informative in the proposed metabolic context. The weakest limitation is that [(Deng et al., 2024-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3c1094cbe0489538a05dbfae684364d473197081) demonstrates a CRISPR metabolic screen in NSCLC under AURKA inhibition, but its pathway conclusions are specific to KEAP1, NRF2, eIF2α-ATF4-ASNS, ASNS, and AURKA-GCN2-eIF2α biology, and it does not provide evidence for ATP5I, F1Fo-ATP synthase, cristae morphology, biguanide target engagement, or NALM-6-specific drug sensitivity.",
  "suggested_action": "The most important action is to adopt a MAGeCK-based analysis pipeline for the NALM-6 screens, using sgRNA read counts from vehicle and treated samples, normalization, mean-variance modeling, sgRNA-level selection tests, and α-RRA gene-level scoring, with FDR control and explicit reporting of log2 fold changes, q-values, and replicate concordance. This should be paired with a pre-specified screen design that uses a well-characterized pooled CRISPR library with multiple sgRNAs per gene, sufficient transduction efficiency, biological replicates, multiple time points, and a reference or untreated control, because [(Li et al., 2014-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3fcd6a0ae6a73c4e196dc13a994d7f12514fc53d) shows robustness to reduced depth and sgRNA number but does not define the exact depth or parameters needed for sublethal drug screens. The next most important action is to define growth-inhibitory rather than fully lethal drug conditions for metformin, rotenone, and oligomycin A using dose-response curves, include vehicle controls, and test whether selection is detectable at multiple time points; if metformin produces weak selection, consider longer screens, higher drug pressure, or complementary CRISPRi/CRISPRa or barcoded clonal competition assays. The third action is to validate top hits and ATP5I-related genes with single-gene knockout lines, rescue experiments, dose-response viability or proliferation assays, AMPK phosphorylation, OCR/ECAR, NAD+/NADH, mitochondrial morphology, and transcriptomic profiling with pathway analysis, following the validation logic in [(Haldrup et al., 2023-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3e2f525ff4f082d565654d86a8e72306d2e1fa1a) and [(Deng et al., 2024-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3c1094cbe0489538a05dbfae684364d473197081). The fourth action is to compare genetic interaction profiles quantitatively rather than by visual top-hit overlap, using rank correlation, difference in MAGeCK RRA scores, pathway enrichment, and known complex I and complex V gene sets, while explicitly testing ATP5I and F1Fo-ATP synthase subunits as positive controls. The least important but still useful action is to run sensitivity analyses comparing MAGeCK with RIGER, RSA, edgeR, or DESeq, and to report sequencing depth, sgRNA coverage, library size, and statistical thresholds to improve reproducibility, as the related works highlight these details but do not provide enough information to transfer them directly.",
